"""The Dynabook, in a field, in colour: Kay's two children in front of a plane.

`dynabook.py` is the 1972 sketch and nothing else — traced ink on white paper.
This is the same trace put somewhere. A small high-wing aeroplane has taxied
into a grass field and settled tail-down with its belly in the grass, and the
two children have got out and sat down in front of it with their machines.

Three things make the two halves one picture rather than a collage.

**The ground line is shared.** The trace draws a rule straight across itself,
from `RULE_TOP` to `RULE_BOT` in its own coordinates — a single stroke inside
the one connected ink loop, so it cannot be removed without cutting the trace
up, which is a reason to use it rather than fight it:
the field's near edge is put exactly there, the rule is extended left and right
at its own weight to the frame's edges, and what was an arbitrary line under two
children becomes the edge of the grass they are sitting in.

**The colour goes under the ink, not into it.** The trace stays verbatim. What
the vectoriser found was one ink loop with forty-one holes in it, and those
holes are already the regions a colourist wants — head, shirt, shorts, face,
hair, screen are each a separate closed loop, because the drawing has an
outline between them. So nothing is re-traced: `REGIONS` names a point inside
each of the twenty-five holes worth naming, and every hole takes the colour of
the point it contains. The other sixteen are slivers between two strokes, never
wide enough for the choice to show, and they take the colour of the nearest
named region. Ink stays ink.

**The plane is drawn the way the children are.** Kay's drawing is entirely
flat — no shading, no perspective, no line weight that varies. So the plane is
flat too: solid shapes, one ink weight, no gradient anywhere on it. Only the
sky and the field are graded, because they are distance rather than objects.

The whole scene is authored in one coordinate space and mapped into whatever
box the node is handed — uniform scale, centred, letterboxed — so it keeps its
shape at any size, the way an image does.
"""

import math
import re

# =======================================================================
# The frame
# =======================================================================

ART_W = 900.0
ART_H = 560.0

# Where the trace is placed in it. The trace is 600x454 in its own space and is
# dropped in at 1:1, so a coordinate below is a trace coordinate plus this.
KIDS_X = 150.0
KIDS_Y = 104.0

# The rule the trace draws across itself runs from y=235.1 to y=242.8 and
# reaches both edges. It is a ruled line under two children sitting on nothing,
# which is wrong once they are sitting in a field, so it is cut out of the ink —
# see `RULE_SPLICES`. What is left is the height they sit at.
HORIZON = 246.0                     # the far hedgerow

# The aeroplane stands on its wheels, and they meet the field low down. Nothing
# drawn in front of it may rise above this line: it is below the belly, the
# cowling and the tailwheel, and leaves only the bottom of the main wheel in the
# grass, where a wheel standing in a field belongs. Blades that began at the
# belly and grew fifteen units were climbing the fuselage instead.
PLANE_GRASS = 350.0

# The rule is an excursion of the one connected ink loop: it leaves the boy's
# back at anchor 129, runs out to the left edge and back by 135, and leaves the
# girl at 210 to do the same to the right by 216. Cutting each run out and
# joining its ends straight removes the line and nothing else. The right-hand
# pair goes first, so the left-hand indices are still valid when it is spliced.
RULE_SPLICES = ((210, 216), (129, 135))

# Kay drew the grass as ink scribbles. This picture has real grass, so they go:
# these loops are free-standing tufts (54 is a hole inside 53), and the tufts
# merged into the children's own bottom edge are cut off at `TUFT_FLOOR` and
# buried under the grass line — which is the only way to part them from the
# outline they were drawn into.
TUFT_LOOPS = {0, 1, 52, 53, 54, 55, 56}

# The tufts drawn into the children's own outline are found instead of listed:
# a tuft is a run of the outline that dives below `TUFT_Y` and comes back, and
# nothing else down there does that. `TUFT_DEPTH` is how far below its own two
# ends a run has to go to count, and `TUFT_SPAN`/`TUFT_WIDTH` keep a cut short
# enough that it can only ever be a tuft and not, say, a leg.
TUFT_Y = 342.0
TUFT_DEPTH = 3.0
TUFT_RISE = 40.0
TUFT_SPAN = 30
TUFT_WIDTH = 150.0

# What is left along the bottom once the tufts are out is still the wobble of
# the hand that drew them — the shorts' hem wanders eight units up and down over
# a few centimetres of paper, which reads as teeth rather than cloth. The run
# below `TUFT_Y` is replaced by a single arc. `HEM_WIDTH` stops a run that
# crosses the whole picture from being flattened into one.
HEM_WIDTH = 360.0

# Neither pass may touch a loop small enough to be swallowed by one: the near
# edge of the girl's machine is seven anchors, five of them along the bottom,
# and smoothing them into three collapsed it to a dash lying in the grass.
SMALL_LOOP = 12

GRASS_LINE = KIDS_Y + 366.0         # where the grass in front of them starts

